WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:03.640
- Perhaps we could reach as high as 57.5.

00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:05.090
- With all due respect, Mr. Brackman,

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:06.990
this is not a negotiation.

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:08.646
- It's definitely a negotiation.

00:00:08.646 --> 00:00:11.229
(bright music)

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:15.500
Hey, Legal Eagles, D. James Stone here

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.050
teaching you how to think like a lawyer

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:19.090
so you can crush law school.

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:22.870
I knew from a very early age
that I wanted to be a lawyer.

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:26.310
And not just any lawyer, a trial lawyer.

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:29.340
And I learned that by
watching lawyers on TV.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.930
Now having been a trial
attorney for over 10 years now,

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:36.150
I see that some shows are
more realistic than others,

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:37.550
so I thought I'd take some time

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:39.670
to review some of the
most famous legal scenes

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:41.300
from TV history.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:45.050
The first scene comes
from the show Ally McBeal.

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:46.990
- Well, as I said, her arm brushed mine.

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:49.880
And when she brushes an arm,
it's with sexual intent.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:51.490
- Objection.
- Just tell us what happened

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:53.790
after she brushed your arm, sir.

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:55.900
- So, that's actually a
really good objection,

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.430
the kind of objection you
would see in most trials.

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:00.570
The witness here is testifying

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:02.880
to things that he has no
personal knowledge of,

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:05.890
which is specifically what
the mental impressions are

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:07.030
of another person.

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:10.300
You can't testify as to what
another person is thinking.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:14.190
So, the objection that's
being made was correct,

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:16.070
and the judge was right to sustain it.

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:17.020
They didn't actually say,

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:19.580
objection, lack of personal
knowledge or speculation,

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:22.420
but the judge understood
that and made the right call.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:24.770
- When I looked at her there was,

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:27.790
I was feeling a little chemistry.

00:01:27.790 --> 00:01:32.790
She, uh, then brought her
hand to my, uh, my, uh--

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:36.500
- So that's a good
recovery by this witness.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:38.140
The judge sustained the objection

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:39.800
to essentially speculation,

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:41.690
and the witness then
proceeds to talk about

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:44.690
only those things that
he personally observed.

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:46.620
And he testifies to two things,

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:49.750
one as to his internal state of mind,

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:51.780
which he's allowed to testify to.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:53.990
And he also testifies as to physical acts

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:55.540
that he actually watched,

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:57.530
he was a percipient witness to.

00:01:57.530 --> 00:01:59.420
An expert can opine about things

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:01.040
that are outside of
their personal knowledge,

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.950
but a lay witness like most witnesses

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:06.380
can only testify to the
things that they have seen,

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.090
or heard, or personally witnessed.

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:10.283
So, that's what this
witness is doing here.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:13.563
- Groin area.

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:18.072
She began to fondle me
a little, and I, well--

00:02:18.072 --> 00:02:20.239
(screams)

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:25.060
- So, sometimes there
are outbursts in court.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:27.500
That's not particularly unusual.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.720
I think the screeching that
the attorney has done here

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:32.020
is a little bit unusual,

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:34.350
and is really bad for his client.

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:38.720
So, he would be better served
by keeping his mouth shut

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.820
and not screeching in a way

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.620
that prejudices his client.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:47.640
Potentially, that's a
prejudicial act to his client,

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:50.210
which sometimes you can't control things,

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:52.110
and things happen.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:56.260
But being professional in court
is a very important thing.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:57.360
You don't wanna lose credibility

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:59.313
in front of the judge or the jury.

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:04.200
And while that particular
yelp was probably unrealistic,

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.830
what is actually more
realistic about this show

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:08.130
compared to most

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:10.320
is that the lawyers are not running around

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:12.010
the well of the court,

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:15.050
the area between the
judge and counsel table

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:16.000
like they own the place.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.680
It appears that the attorney
who is asking questions

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.240
is right in front of counsel table.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:25.090
And usually if you're
conducting an examination,

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:27.340
you're not gonna be right
up against the witness,

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:29.940
you're not gonna be pacing
up and down the well.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:32.390
You're gonna be either at counsel table

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:33.900
or you're gonna be behind it,

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:36.120
and here it appears that the attorney

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:37.820
is just in front of counsel table,

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:39.610
which is usually allowed.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:41.340
You have to ask permission of the judge

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:43.230
if you are going to enter the well.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:45.570
But they're not doing
something that's crazy

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:47.300
like running around, yelling,

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:49.160
like you'll see in a lot
of procedural dramas.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:51.200
So, that part is actually realistic,

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:54.380
the yelp is probably not so realistic.

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:57.640
The next scene is from Boston Legal.

00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:59.770
- Routinely go to church on Sundays

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:03.467
and ceremoniously eat the body
of Christ, drink his blood.

00:04:03.467 --> 00:04:06.957
"He who eats my flesh and
drinks my blood abides in me,

00:04:06.957 --> 00:04:09.810
"and I in him," Jesus said that.

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:11.300
- How dare you.

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:12.300
- What?

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:13.170
Who have I offended now?

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:16.160
- How dare you take a
holy sacrament literally?

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:17.033
- Is that not done?

00:04:17.033 --> 00:04:21.033
- You have equated it with
a vile and despicable act.

00:04:23.290 --> 00:04:25.610
- Okay, so here a couple
things are going on.

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:28.820
It appears that the attorney
is giving a closing argument

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:32.220
about a client who has

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:34.690
conducted an act of cannibalism,

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:37.288
and he is defending that by saying

00:04:37.288 --> 00:04:40.010
that there are other religious orders

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:44.000
that conduct an act that
is similar to cannibalism.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:46.620
The judge here has done essentially

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:48.730
the worst thing you can do as a judge

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:53.270
which is to opine personally
against another attorney.

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:54.810
So, he's taken offense at something

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:56.420
that the attorney has said.

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:58.690
He is not making a legal argument,

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:01.680
he is simply just issuing
his own personal thoughts

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:03.780
about what is or is not allowable

00:05:04.630 --> 00:05:06.913
according to the scripture.

00:05:09.353 --> 00:05:11.440
That's the worst thing
that a judge can do.

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:13.440
A judge is supposed to be impartial,

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:18.320
and if you have lost your impartiality,

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:19.800
and it's clear to the jury

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:23.200
that you are no longer impartial,

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:24.560
then it's your duty as a judge

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:27.033
probably to declare a mistrial,

00:05:27.033 --> 00:05:30.760
and potentially there could be some

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:32.410
professional responsibility actions

00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:33.570
taken against the judge,

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:37.100
because he's shown that
he cannot be impartial.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:41.360
The next step that I would
do if I was an attorney

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:46.360
is move to dismiss the
judge for being prejudiced,

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:48.520
and I would use the record of the court

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:51.310
to show this lack of impartiality.

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:55.290
Potentially that's the plan
that this attorney has done

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:59.390
is to show that the judge
is not being impartial

00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:03.410
and trying to get some
favor from the jury.

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:06.040
But a long-standing judge,

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:08.570
as it appears this judge would be,

00:06:08.570 --> 00:06:11.310
would never make these sort of statements,

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:15.233
and if they did they could
be removed from the office.

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:20.790
- I apologize, Your Honor.

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:24.260
I certainly don't mean
to indict Holy Communion.

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:27.700
But let's not carry on

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:31.730
with the idea that the
notion of cannibalism

00:06:31.730 --> 00:06:34.940
offends the sanctity of life.

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:37.610
It has roots not only in sacraments,

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:41.340
not to be taken literally,
but also Greek mythology.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:43.250
It's still glorified in certain sections

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:46.880
of both the South and I believe Malibu.

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:49.000
And I might add, it's not illegal.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:53.370
- So, the lawyer here is
making an impassioned plea,

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:55.080
which you're allowed to do to the jury,

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:58.070
but in so doing it sounds
like he's bringing in

00:06:58.070 --> 00:07:00.070
a lot of facts and figures

00:07:00.070 --> 00:07:03.640
that almost certainly did
not come out in trial.

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:06.090
And it's really important for the jury

00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:11.090
only to use facts that came out in trial

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:12.680
to base their decision.

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:15.780
And all of these statistics,

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:20.040
it's possible that the lawyer

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:23.060
brought them in through a
process called judicial notice,

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:25.350
which is that sometimes
there are statistics

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:28.760
that are so incontrovertible
that the court

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:31.260
will just allow them to
be brought into court

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:33.530
without showing an underlying basis.

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:37.330
For example, if you were going to say that

00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:40.080
a certain date occurred on a certain day,

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:42.560
like a Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday,

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:45.560
you could show that on a calendar,

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:47.940
and that fact would be so incontrovertible

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:49.540
that you're allowed to bring that in.

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:52.320
These statistics about cannibalism

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:57.320
and about the notion of who and who is not

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:01.040
cared for by the government,

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:03.370
that seems like a pretty far stretch,

00:08:03.370 --> 00:08:06.150
and it would be improper
for him to cloud the record

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:09.860
with these extrinsic facts
that did not come out.

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:13.542
The next scene comes from L.A. Law.

00:08:13.542 --> 00:08:15.680
- UCLA, Harvard Law.

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:16.513
- Ooh, UCLA.

00:08:16.513 --> 00:08:17.860
- Editor of the Law Review.

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:22.160
Ames Competition, Best Oralist.

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:27.560
- So, the Ames Competition
is a real life competition

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:31.190
at Harvard Law that is for a moot court.

00:08:31.190 --> 00:08:33.150
And they're saying that this person

00:08:33.150 --> 00:08:36.650
won Best Oralist at the
moot court competition.

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:38.380
Moot court is where attorneys

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:42.460
pretend to be appellate
attorneys, not trial attorneys,

00:08:42.460 --> 00:08:46.650
and they handle a fake
appellate court case

00:08:46.650 --> 00:08:48.280
in front of a panel of judges.

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:51.460
And the Ames moot court
competition is very famous.

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:53.300
They are so famous, in fact,

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:54.960
that they often get Supreme Court judges

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:57.230
to judge the competitors.

00:08:57.230 --> 00:09:02.230
So, that's a fun real life
fact that they threw in there.

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:05.220
- [Leland] Oh my, I'm
impressed, Mr. Rawlins.

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:06.273
- Thank you, sir.

00:09:07.371 --> 00:09:10.240
- An attorney could often
be impressed by that resume.

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:13.300
He went to UCLA for
undergrad, Harvard Law School,

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:15.130
won the moot court competition,

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:18.330
and he's now interviewing
for a job in Los Angeles.

00:09:18.330 --> 00:09:21.430
That's not a bad resume to bring in there.

00:09:21.430 --> 00:09:23.230
- Tell us a bit about yourself.

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:25.920
- Well, let's see, my
mother's an attorney,

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:29.000
my father's a professor of
economics as UC Riverside.

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:30.920
I've clerked for the past few summers

00:09:30.920 --> 00:09:32.040
at Felder, Ivy, McCoy.

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:34.640
As a matter of fact, they
offered me a permanent position,

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:36.790
but under the heading less is more,

00:09:36.790 --> 00:09:38.390
I think that a small prestigious firm

00:09:38.390 --> 00:09:40.160
such as McKenzie, Brackman

00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:42.110
could be more responsive
to my personal needs,

00:09:42.110 --> 00:09:43.040
or at least Bill Howard--

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:44.400
- That's really interesting.

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:47.430
So, that is a very realistic way

00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:49.400
of going about getting
a job in law school.

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:53.010
This seems to be a recent graduate

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:54.350
from Harvard Law School.

00:09:54.350 --> 00:09:58.870
He said he clerked for a
few years at a law firm.

00:09:58.870 --> 00:10:00.580
I don't think the law firm is real,

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:05.350
but often the way you get a
job in law school is you clerk,

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:08.740
it's called a summer
associateship with a law firm,

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:12.500
and if you do a good
job in your 2L summer,

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:16.270
then they will often offer
you a job upon graduation.

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:20.210
And what this young man
is saying he's doing

00:10:20.210 --> 00:10:24.090
is he doesn't wanna work for
this big prestigious law firm.

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:26.130
Instead he wants to come out

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:28.320
and interview with this boutique law firm,

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:30.800
because it suits his whatever it is

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:32.910
that he's looking for in Los Angeles.

00:10:32.910 --> 00:10:36.990
So, so far a pretty realistic reason

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:39.760
for this young man to apply

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:43.150
for a job at this law
firm, so far so good.

00:10:43.150 --> 00:10:44.690
- At least Bill Howard thinks so.

00:10:44.690 --> 00:10:47.360
- Bill Howard as in Howard Enterprises?

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:48.193
- Yes, sir.

00:10:48.193 --> 00:10:51.040
He's been a close, personal
friend of my family for years.

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:52.280
- Oh boy.

00:10:52.280 --> 00:10:54.680
I don't think it's particularly realistic

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:56.890
to drop names in the interview.

00:10:56.890 --> 00:11:00.180
That might go unsaid, but to bring it up

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:04.940
and to boast about your family connections

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:06.275
in the middle of the interview,

00:11:06.275 --> 00:11:08.770
that's not good form.

00:11:08.770 --> 00:11:13.100
It happens, it probably happens
more often than it should.

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:15.320
People are probably better served

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:19.070
by getting in through
merit than connections.

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:22.510
But, you know, sometimes
family connections matter.

00:11:22.510 --> 00:11:25.073
It's a part of the world.

00:11:26.810 --> 00:11:27.643
- Thank you.

00:11:27.643 --> 00:11:30.160
- What would you be looking
for in the way of compensation?

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:33.040
- 72.5, and I wouldn't be opposed

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:34.480
to your designating a percentage of that

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:35.630
as a signing bonus.

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:39.020
- Excuse me, but we've been
starting our associates

00:11:39.020 --> 00:11:41.020
pretty much commensurate
with the going rate,

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:42.900
which as I'm sure you're aware,

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:44.290
is considerably lower.

00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:45.123
- I understand, sir,

00:11:45.123 --> 00:11:47.620
but I've already been
offered 71 with Horton, Gold.

00:11:47.620 --> 00:11:49.883
- So much for your
doctrine of less is more.

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:53.583
- Sometimes more is more.

00:11:54.490 --> 00:11:56.390
- This is really interesting.

00:11:56.390 --> 00:12:00.230
It's not often that junior associates

00:12:00.230 --> 00:12:02.730
bargain so hard for their salary.

00:12:02.730 --> 00:12:04.870
But what he's saying is that the firm

00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:08.650
that he was a summer associate
at, and he clerked there,

00:12:08.650 --> 00:12:10.840
has already given him a
job offer, which is normal.

00:12:10.840 --> 00:12:12.610
He spent his summer there,

00:12:12.610 --> 00:12:15.010
and they gave him an
offer upon graduation.

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:16.180
He's trying to leverage that

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:19.120
to get a better salary at this law firm.

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:24.100
And most law firms do actually adhere to

00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:26.570
sort of a standard rate.

00:12:26.570 --> 00:12:30.940
This is in the early
'90s or the late '80s,

00:12:30.940 --> 00:12:35.020
and they're talking about
salaries of $70,000 or more.

00:12:35.020 --> 00:12:38.270
That seems about right for the time.

00:12:38.270 --> 00:12:42.490
These days, graduates
from prestigious law firms

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:47.140
going into the top notch law firms

00:12:47.140 --> 00:12:49.673
are likely to get a salary of between

00:12:49.673 --> 00:12:52.610
160,000 and $190,000 per year,

00:12:52.610 --> 00:12:55.370
and there's sort of a standard
scale that is applied.

00:12:55.370 --> 00:12:58.480
And most law firms say
that they are lockstep

00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:00.070
so you don't have any bargaining power.

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:03.940
They pay all first year
associates X amount,

00:13:03.940 --> 00:13:05.650
between 160 and 190,

00:13:05.650 --> 00:13:08.510
and he's trying to do
his best to get even more

00:13:08.510 --> 00:13:09.660
out of this firm.

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:11.320
So, good for him,

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:14.780
I wish it was possible
for more law students

00:13:14.780 --> 00:13:16.090
to bargain more,

00:13:16.090 --> 00:13:19.060
but sadly most law firms are lockstep,

00:13:19.060 --> 00:13:21.423
and not a lot of bargaining power there.

00:13:23.470 --> 00:13:28.210
- Well, perhaps we could
reach as high as 57.5.

00:13:28.210 --> 00:13:29.113
- With all due respect, Mr. Brackman,

00:13:29.113 --> 00:13:31.580
this is not a negotiation.

00:13:31.580 --> 00:13:33.180
- It's definitely a negotiation.

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:37.590
- Gentlemen, it's been a
genuine honor and a pleasure.

00:13:37.590 --> 00:13:38.763
Thank you very much.

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:42.090
- He's a winner.

00:13:42.090 --> 00:13:43.590
I want him to be our winner.

00:13:43.590 --> 00:13:45.483
- I strongly disagree.

00:13:47.030 --> 00:13:48.300
- I'm pulling rank, Douglas.

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:49.133
- What are you gonna do

00:13:49.133 --> 00:13:51.130
when his salary becomes common knowledge

00:13:51.130 --> 00:13:52.635
among the other associates.

00:13:52.635 --> 00:13:54.035
- We'll see that it doesn't.

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:57.540
- Ooh, that's true.

00:13:57.540 --> 00:14:01.201
Because law firms ostensibly pay

00:14:01.201 --> 00:14:03.350
first year associates all the same,

00:14:03.350 --> 00:14:04.640
if it became public knowledge

00:14:04.640 --> 00:14:06.320
that one associate was paid

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:08.210
significantly more than the rest,

00:14:08.210 --> 00:14:10.140
that would cause some dissent.

00:14:10.140 --> 00:14:12.193
The next clip comes from Boston Legal.

00:14:20.210 --> 00:14:24.933
- Value of human life, it's a bugger.

00:14:26.530 --> 00:14:28.120
My friend Jerry Spence tells a story

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:30.470
of this paperboy delivering his papers

00:14:30.470 --> 00:14:31.550
on his morning route one day.

00:14:31.550 --> 00:14:33.580
He throws the paper through the window,

00:14:33.580 --> 00:14:37.123
shatters the glass, blinds a woman.

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:41.630
What's the value of that?

00:14:44.640 --> 00:14:47.430
Paperboy decides to give the woman

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:50.360
his profits for the week,

00:14:50.360 --> 00:14:52.850
not very much considering she was blinded.

00:14:52.850 --> 00:14:57.850
Now here we have a loss of
life, an avoidable loss.

00:14:57.900 --> 00:15:02.243
Not merely an accident, one
they could have prevented.

00:15:03.390 --> 00:15:05.910
I think the paperboy had the right idea.

00:15:05.910 --> 00:15:06.900
At the end of this trial,

00:15:06.900 --> 00:15:07.980
I'm gonna get up.

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:10.730
I'm gonna ask the
defendant to pay my client

00:15:10.730 --> 00:15:11.803
one week's profit.

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:16.030
Let's make that a day's profit.

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:18.180
Let's not get greedy, here.

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:21.080
We may not be able to put a
price Mary Finnerty's life,

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:24.493
but you can at least pay her family

00:15:24.493 --> 00:15:27.470
the profits for the day they killed her.

00:15:27.470 --> 00:15:29.270
- So, there's a couple
things going on here,

00:15:29.270 --> 00:15:30.130
which are pretty interesting.

00:15:30.130 --> 00:15:33.650
Number one is that there are almost always

00:15:33.650 --> 00:15:37.660
two phases to major personal injury cases.

00:15:37.660 --> 00:15:39.200
Sometimes they are tried together,

00:15:39.200 --> 00:15:40.790
sometimes they are tried separately,

00:15:40.790 --> 00:15:41.990
and there are reasons for that.

00:15:41.990 --> 00:15:45.620
And the two things that you
have to show is liability,

00:15:45.620 --> 00:15:47.860
which means that the defendant

00:15:47.860 --> 00:15:50.850
is responsible for the
injuries of the plaintiff,

00:15:50.850 --> 00:15:52.490
but then separate from that is,

00:15:52.490 --> 00:15:56.300
how much compensation should
the defendant pay the plaintiff

00:15:56.300 --> 00:15:57.470
if they are liable.

00:15:57.470 --> 00:16:01.063
And those are often called
liability versus compensation.

00:16:01.900 --> 00:16:04.730
So, here the attorney is pretty confident,

00:16:04.730 --> 00:16:07.850
or perhaps they've already
established liability,

00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:09.740
and so they're simply going to

00:16:09.740 --> 00:16:11.730
the damages portion of the trial.

00:16:11.730 --> 00:16:16.166
And what he's hinting at
is how do you compensate

00:16:16.166 --> 00:16:21.120
a plaintiff for the injury
that they have received.

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:24.900
And in the law there are
two ways of going about it.

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:27.720
One is pure compensation for the injury,

00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:29.360
so however much you're injured,

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:31.100
you're gonna get a monetary sum

00:16:31.100 --> 00:16:33.070
that is equal to that amount.

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:35.920
But if the defendant's actions

00:16:35.920 --> 00:16:40.610
are so egregious that
they warrant punishment,

00:16:40.610 --> 00:16:42.450
it's rare in civil cases,

00:16:42.450 --> 00:16:43.750
but sometimes you're allowed

00:16:43.750 --> 00:16:45.310
what are called punitive damages.

00:16:45.310 --> 00:16:48.640
And that's what William
Shatner here is hinting at.

00:16:48.640 --> 00:16:51.710
He's saying that as a punitive measure,

00:16:51.710 --> 00:16:54.860
the defendant should
be required to disgorge

00:16:54.860 --> 00:16:59.860
a single day's profit for the
injuries that they caused.

00:16:59.860 --> 00:17:02.800
And I don't know what
law is being broken here

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:06.010
that would allow him to
get punitive damages.

00:17:06.010 --> 00:17:09.050
Punitive damages are somewhat rare.

00:17:09.050 --> 00:17:12.660
They usually require some
sort of egregious conduct.

00:17:12.660 --> 00:17:14.940
But if you are allowed punitive damages,

00:17:14.940 --> 00:17:16.250
sometimes they're set by law

00:17:16.250 --> 00:17:19.670
and sometimes they are
just simply a fraction

00:17:19.670 --> 00:17:22.030
that's set by the judge or the jury

00:17:22.030 --> 00:17:24.590
of the worth of the defendant,

00:17:24.590 --> 00:17:28.320
or sometimes even the profit
that they would have received

00:17:28.320 --> 00:17:30.903
in a day, or a month, or a year.

00:17:33.310 --> 00:17:35.470
The punitive damages should be

00:17:35.470 --> 00:17:38.310
large enough to punish the defendant.

00:17:38.310 --> 00:17:41.400
So, if you're dealing with
a really wealthy defendant,

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:45.900
then it needs to be large
enough to punish them for them.

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:48.510
Because sometimes if you have

00:17:48.510 --> 00:17:49.950
what would seem like a large amount,

00:17:49.950 --> 00:17:52.190
if you're dealing with a
multi-billion dollar corporation,

00:17:52.190 --> 00:17:54.650
it doesn't really punish them

00:17:54.650 --> 00:17:56.090
in a way that they're
going to take notice.

00:17:56.090 --> 00:17:58.730
So, that's what William
Shatner's going after here.

00:17:58.730 --> 00:18:01.900
A day's profit to really make them

00:18:01.900 --> 00:18:04.773
feel the pain that they
inflicted on the plaintiff here.

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:09.130
- What could be more fair than that?

00:18:09.130 --> 00:18:10.610
- What could be more fair?

00:18:10.610 --> 00:18:11.443
So fair.

00:18:14.470 --> 00:18:17.050
- My client's profit's are not relevant.

00:18:17.050 --> 00:18:20.000
The number of stores they
have is not relevant.

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:21.450
How dare you go into that?

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:23.853
And how dare you not shut him down?

00:18:25.090 --> 00:18:30.090
- So, whether or not the
defendant's profits are relevant

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:34.460
would be decided long
before they get to trial.

00:18:34.460 --> 00:18:36.690
There would be motions that would

00:18:36.690 --> 00:18:39.150
lay the foundation for why

00:18:39.150 --> 00:18:43.230
a corporation's profits are
relevant to punitive damage.

00:18:43.230 --> 00:18:47.330
There would be a slew of pretrial motions

00:18:47.330 --> 00:18:50.670
to decide whether or not the profits

00:18:50.670 --> 00:18:52.970
and the punitive damages of a company

00:18:52.970 --> 00:18:55.163
are relevant to the case or not.

00:18:56.120 --> 00:18:59.630
If indeed there has
been no motion for that

00:18:59.630 --> 00:19:02.460
and there has been no back and forth

00:19:02.460 --> 00:19:04.220
between the attorneys and the judge,

00:19:04.220 --> 00:19:06.670
then it would've been incredibly improper

00:19:06.670 --> 00:19:09.330
for William Shatner to bring
that up in front of the jury.

00:19:09.330 --> 00:19:11.670
Potentially even leading to a mistrial.

00:19:11.670 --> 00:19:14.850
But what TV shows often get wrong

00:19:14.850 --> 00:19:18.010
is the mountain of paperwork
that happens before trial.

00:19:18.010 --> 00:19:20.700
All of these decisions,
all these legal questions,

00:19:20.700 --> 00:19:22.850
are settled long before you get to court.

00:19:22.850 --> 00:19:26.400
They would not have a sidebar
like what's going on here

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:28.840
to decide, in the middle of trial,

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:31.260
whether he's allowed to
make these arguments or not.

00:19:31.260 --> 00:19:33.530
That's totally unrealistic.

00:19:33.530 --> 00:19:35.741
- May I go off the record briefly?

00:19:35.741 --> 00:19:37.080
- Go right ahead.

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:39.903
- Thank you, this is not
for your ears, by the way.

00:19:41.550 --> 00:19:44.410
You stupid bastard, cases rarely turn on

00:19:44.410 --> 00:19:45.680
what's legally relevant.

00:19:45.680 --> 00:19:47.370
What you have to be concerned with here

00:19:47.370 --> 00:19:50.320
is if All Shop makes $160 million

00:19:50.320 --> 00:19:52.433
of pure profit a day, a day,

00:19:52.433 --> 00:19:54.370
a jury will get wind of those numbers.

00:19:54.370 --> 00:19:56.290
- In which case we'll appeal.

00:19:56.290 --> 00:19:57.310
- Great, and do you think you'll be

00:19:57.310 --> 00:19:58.970
the lawyers to handle that appeal?

00:19:58.970 --> 00:20:02.400
When your client gets whacked
with a $50 million judgment?

00:20:02.400 --> 00:20:06.170
You're an ass, you're
an ass, you're an ass.

00:20:06.170 --> 00:20:08.400
- So, a couple things going on here.

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:10.480
He's basically admitted that

00:20:11.330 --> 00:20:14.030
the profits of the
defendant are not relevant,

00:20:14.030 --> 00:20:15.550
not something you want to do

00:20:15.550 --> 00:20:18.110
if you are trying to get those profits

00:20:18.110 --> 00:20:20.300
in front of the jury.

00:20:20.300 --> 00:20:22.270
Probably not a good idea
to admit to the judge

00:20:22.270 --> 00:20:24.000
that they're not relevant,

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:26.300
because the judge has a duty to make sure

00:20:26.300 --> 00:20:30.873
that no irrelevant evidence
comes before the jury.

00:20:31.870 --> 00:20:33.820
William Shatner does hint at something

00:20:33.820 --> 00:20:34.840
that is often the case,

00:20:34.840 --> 00:20:38.470
which is that the same
lawyers that tried the case

00:20:38.470 --> 00:20:40.540
are rarely the ones to take the appeal.

00:20:40.540 --> 00:20:41.980
Sometimes they do, sometimes they don't.

00:20:41.980 --> 00:20:44.640
There are specialists
who handle the appeal.

00:20:44.640 --> 00:20:46.440
But the opposing attorney
is absolutely right,

00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:49.620
that if he brings in
these prejudicial facts

00:20:49.620 --> 00:20:52.390
against the will of the court,

00:20:52.390 --> 00:20:57.390
then he has a dead winner on
appeal, or at least he should.

00:20:57.440 --> 00:21:00.430
Now, whether they're gonna
wanna go through the cost

00:21:00.430 --> 00:21:04.630
of going through another
trial again after the appeal,

00:21:04.630 --> 00:21:05.870
that's a bigger question,

00:21:05.870 --> 00:21:07.590
but it sounds like a big company.

00:21:07.590 --> 00:21:11.270
And they may want to go
through all that again

00:21:11.270 --> 00:21:13.000
on the idea that the plaintiff's attorneys

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:15.940
won't wanna go through
the trial one more time,

00:21:15.940 --> 00:21:17.750
and potentially lose.

00:21:17.750 --> 00:21:19.960
So, some of that is realistic,

00:21:19.960 --> 00:21:22.950
but this whole monologue
in front of the judge,

00:21:22.950 --> 00:21:25.730
that is not gonna happen.

00:21:25.730 --> 00:21:29.150
The next clip comes from Rick and Morty.

00:21:29.150 --> 00:21:31.270
- [Narrator] You are about
to enter the courtroom

00:21:31.270 --> 00:21:33.550
of Judge Morty Durham, Jr.

00:21:33.550 --> 00:21:36.240
The people are real, the cases are real,

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:38.230
the rulings are final.

00:21:38.230 --> 00:21:40.250
This is his courtroom.

00:21:40.250 --> 00:21:42.513
This is Judge Morty.

00:21:43.390 --> 00:21:45.350
60-year-old inmate Rick Allen

00:21:45.350 --> 00:21:46.940
is being accused of committing murder

00:21:46.940 --> 00:21:49.760
of a fellow inmate at
Georgia Penitentiary.

00:21:49.760 --> 00:21:52.170
Mr. Allen claims that he is being setup,

00:21:52.170 --> 00:21:54.630
and that his lawyer is
requesting sexual favors

00:21:54.630 --> 00:21:56.903
in exchange for proper legal advice.

00:21:57.880 --> 00:22:01.640
- Okay, (clears throat) okay, Mr. Allen.

00:22:01.640 --> 00:22:02.473
- Right.

00:22:02.473 --> 00:22:04.930
- I believe that, I
believe that your case is,

00:22:04.930 --> 00:22:07.870
was going to be going to
trial not this Monday,

00:22:07.870 --> 00:22:09.380
but the following Monday.

00:22:09.380 --> 00:22:10.560
Do you understand that?

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:11.393
- Yes.

00:22:11.393 --> 00:22:14.120
- Okay, I tell you, if you
need to tell me something,

00:22:14.120 --> 00:22:16.650
I want, I'll let you come
up here to the podium

00:22:16.650 --> 00:22:18.160
so you can speak into the microphone.

00:22:18.160 --> 00:22:21.060
- So my understanding is that this clip

00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:24.690
is actually a real life transcript

00:22:24.690 --> 00:22:26.950
that happened in a criminal proceeding,

00:22:26.950 --> 00:22:29.250
that the makers of Rick and Morty

00:22:29.250 --> 00:22:34.010
then set to the voices of Rick
and Morty and animated it.

00:22:34.010 --> 00:22:37.890
And it really sounds like
some of the crazy stuff

00:22:37.890 --> 00:22:41.290
that can happen in criminal proceedings.

00:22:41.290 --> 00:22:44.130
What's happening here is not uncommon.

00:22:44.130 --> 00:22:47.450
Often the defendant will not be happy

00:22:47.450 --> 00:22:50.400
with his publicly appointed attorney,

00:22:50.400 --> 00:22:52.870
and he or she will request

00:22:52.870 --> 00:22:56.450
that that attorney be either substituted,

00:22:56.450 --> 00:22:58.480
or that the defendant is allowed

00:22:58.480 --> 00:23:00.380
to just handle things on their own.

00:23:00.380 --> 00:23:01.960
And that's a thing that happens,

00:23:01.960 --> 00:23:05.000
it's called representing yourself pro se,

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:07.520
when you represent yourself.

00:23:07.520 --> 00:23:09.540
And there's an old adage that

00:23:09.540 --> 00:23:12.660
anyone who represents themself
has a fool for a client.

00:23:12.660 --> 00:23:15.340
And the judges don't like it.

00:23:15.340 --> 00:23:17.950
You're not, generally,
gonna do a very good job,

00:23:17.950 --> 00:23:19.500
even if you've been
trained as an attorney,

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:21.620
which most people have not.

00:23:21.620 --> 00:23:24.300
But judges hate it because
it slows things down,

00:23:24.300 --> 00:23:26.130
they really have to hold the hands

00:23:26.130 --> 00:23:29.220
of the defendants who
represent themselves.

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:31.650
And it can really just muck things up,

00:23:31.650 --> 00:23:34.140
which I'm guessing is what happens here.

00:23:34.140 --> 00:23:35.700
- You have a right to an attorney.

00:23:35.700 --> 00:23:36.533
- This lawyer has--

00:23:36.533 --> 00:23:38.330
- You don't have a right
to a specific attorney.

00:23:38.330 --> 00:23:40.820
- This lawyer has made
sexual advances on me.

00:23:40.820 --> 00:23:42.850
He's misrepresenting my case.

00:23:42.850 --> 00:23:45.880
He told me if, if I wanted
him to do a good job,

00:23:45.880 --> 00:23:47.470
I had to let him give me oral sex.

00:23:47.470 --> 00:23:48.470
He's had doctors--

00:23:48.470 --> 00:23:49.830
- Oh boy.

00:23:49.830 --> 00:23:53.960
So, defendants will say
a lot of crazy things

00:23:53.960 --> 00:23:56.020
to try and get their way,

00:23:56.020 --> 00:24:01.020
and I'm guessing the attorney
did not make sexual advances.

00:24:01.560 --> 00:24:04.090
I'm thinking that this guy is just

00:24:04.090 --> 00:24:08.080
saying whatever he can to
get a different attorney.

00:24:08.080 --> 00:24:10.810
- He's had doctors at Central
State Hospital put a--

00:24:10.810 --> 00:24:13.070
- He's had doctors at
Central State Hospital?

00:24:13.070 --> 00:24:14.620
- Put a false diagnosis on me.

00:24:14.620 --> 00:24:16.460
- Okay, I don't, you know,

00:24:16.460 --> 00:24:18.140
I know Mr. Wyatt pretty well,

00:24:18.140 --> 00:24:19.590
and I don't think he has the ability

00:24:19.590 --> 00:24:22.380
to make doctors at
Central State do anything.

00:24:22.380 --> 00:24:23.840
- Well, they did it and he's the one

00:24:23.840 --> 00:24:24.710
that had me sent down there.

00:24:24.710 --> 00:24:26.300
- Well, you know, they may have done it,

00:24:26.300 --> 00:24:29.660
but I don't think that he
had anything to do with it.

00:24:29.660 --> 00:24:32.067
- Alright, well, he won't
give me the discovery.

00:24:32.067 --> 00:24:33.760
There's things in the discovery,

00:24:33.760 --> 00:24:35.770
he's supposed to give me the incident,

00:24:35.770 --> 00:24:38.340
and told me that it was the discovery.

00:24:38.340 --> 00:24:39.173
- Okay.

00:24:39.173 --> 00:24:41.390
- They're alternating
documents that I've read.

00:24:41.390 --> 00:24:42.910
- If you want--

00:24:42.910 --> 00:24:45.630
- Alright, so here there's a ring of truth

00:24:45.630 --> 00:24:47.700
in what the defendant is saying.

00:24:47.700 --> 00:24:49.320
The prosecution has an obligation

00:24:49.320 --> 00:24:52.220
to disclose all of their
evidence to the defendant,

00:24:52.220 --> 00:24:54.570
and that includes documentary evidence,

00:24:54.570 --> 00:24:56.930
that includes what witnesses
they're going to call

00:24:56.930 --> 00:24:58.803
against that person at trial.

00:25:00.870 --> 00:25:03.310
Gone are the days where
either the prosecution

00:25:03.310 --> 00:25:05.960
or the defense could
ambush the other side.

00:25:05.960 --> 00:25:08.584
You have to disclose all
of that ahead of time,

00:25:08.584 --> 00:25:11.340
and so what this defendant is claiming

00:25:11.340 --> 00:25:14.180
is that he didn't receive
some of that evidence,

00:25:14.180 --> 00:25:16.540
and that he wants it.

00:25:16.540 --> 00:25:18.910
And if that is indeed the case,

00:25:18.910 --> 00:25:22.130
it's probably not the case
because this guy sounds crazy,

00:25:22.130 --> 00:25:24.330
if there is something
that has been withheld

00:25:25.290 --> 00:25:28.040
the prosecution needs to
turn that over to him.

00:25:28.040 --> 00:25:31.830
Alright, so those are some of
my favorite legal TV shows.

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:34.210
I can see some of those
situations happening in real life,

00:25:34.210 --> 00:25:36.470
but others would get
the attorneys disbarred

00:25:36.470 --> 00:25:39.160
and probably get the judges
thrown off the bench.

